  <h1>India’s Migration Trends — Internal Mobility and Policy Gaps</h1>
  <div><em>Indian Express | Date: 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>Migration in India is both a driver of development and a reflection of socio-economic inequalities. In 2024, Indian Express reported on rising internal migration due to urbanisation, climate change, and economic aspirations, while policy frameworks remain fragmented. For UPSC, migration highlights the linkages of society, economy, and governance.
________________________________________
Current Trends
•	Internal Migrants (Census 2011): ~45 crore (37% of population). Updated numbers likely higher (~50 crore).
•	Types: Rural–urban, urban–urban, seasonal, and distress migration.
•	Gender Dimension: Women migrants (55%) largely due to marriage, but rising workforce migration too.
•	Remittances: Critical for rural households; intrastate remittances supplement agriculture.
•	Climate-Induced Migration: Rising due to floods, droughts, and heat stress.
________________________________________
Drivers of Migration
1.	Economic Opportunities: Cities as hubs of jobs in construction, services.
2.	Agrarian Distress: Low farm incomes push rural workers to migrate.
3.	Education & Health: Families move for better facilities.
4.	Climate Change: Environmental shocks creating “climate refugees.”
5.	Social Aspirations: Young workers seeking upward mobility.
________________________________________
Challenges
1.	Informality: Migrant workers largely in unorganised sectors with no social security.
2.	Exclusion: Often excluded from PDS, health, and housing benefits.
3.	Exploitation: Poor working conditions, wage theft, gender harassment.
4.	Documentation Gaps: Lack of portability of entitlements across states.
5.	Urban Stress: Housing, slums, infrastructure burden in cities.
________________________________________
Government Measures
•	One Nation, One Ration Card (ONORC): Portability of PDS entitlements.
•	e-Shram Portal: National database for unorganised workers.
•	Inter-State Migrant Workmen Act (1979): Poorly enforced.
•	PM Awas Yojana (Urban): Affordable housing push.
•	Labour Codes (2020): Provide for social security portability (implementation pending).
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Achievements<br/>•	COVID-19 Lessons: Policies for migrant workers prioritised after 2020 crisis.<br/>•	Digital Reforms: e-Shram registered ~30 crore workers by 2024.<br/>•	Civil Society Role: NGOs active in health and legal aid for migrants.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Significance<br/>•	Economic Growth: Migrants power construction, manufacturing, and services.<br/>•	Social Justice: Migrant rights essential for equity.<br/>•	Urbanisation: Migration central to India’s demographic transition.<br/>•	Political Impact: Voting rights and migrant inclusion emerging issues.<br/>________________________________________<br/>UPSC Relevance<br/>•	GS-I (Society): Urbanisation, population movements.<br/>•	GS-II (Governance): Welfare delivery, labour policies.<br/>•	GS-III (Economy): Employment, informal sector.<br/>•	Essay:<br/>o	“Migration is not a crisis but a reality of development.”<br/>o	“Migrant workers are the invisible backbone of Indian cities.”<br/>•	PSIR Paper II: Citizenship, rights, and social contract.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Strengthen ONORC: Ensure universal portability of all entitlements.<br/>2.	Expand Social Security: Health, insurance, pensions for migrants.<br/>3.	Safe Working Conditions: Enforce labour codes, inspections.<br/>4.	Urban Planning: Migrant-friendly housing, sanitation, and transport.<br/>5.	Climate Adaptation: Address push factors in climate-vulnerable regions.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>Migration reflects India’s development paradox — it drives economic growth while exposing social vulnerabilities. Ensuring dignity and rights for migrants is central to inclusive development. For UPSC aspirants, it illustrates the convergence of economy, society, and governance.</p>
